<Project>

  <!-- F8 (final whole-branch review, 2026-08-05). The build-output guard ONLY - deliberately
       nothing else.

       Why this file exists: MSBuild walks UP from each project and stops at the FIRST
       Directory.Build.props, so src/Directory.Build.props was never reached from tests\. The
       common routes were caught transitively (a test project's src\ ProjectReferences inherit the
       global property and error), but
         dotnet build tests/LocalScribe.App.Tests (with the "no-dependencies" flag) -o C:\temp\x
       evaluates no src\ project at all - so no guard ran, the test assembly landed outside the
       repository, and RepoPaths.SolutionRoot() (tests/LocalScribe.App.Tests/XamlHygieneTests.cs)
       walked up past the repo into whatever .git it found first. "Silently validates a different
       tree" is rationale #1 in the guard's own comment block, and it is about THIS assembly.

       Why ONLY the guard: src/Directory.Build.props also carries the product version property
       (0.9.0) and the git-sha stamp target. Those belong to the SHIPPING assemblies -
       Assembly.GetName().Version lands in every session.json as evidentiary, append-only data.
       Test assemblies must not be stamped with a product version, so this file imports the shared
       guard and inherits nothing else. Keeping the guard in build\BuildOutputGuard.targets means
       ONE copy of the target logic; two copies could drift apart, which is the failure mode the
       shared ShutdownFlush.Timeout constant was introduced for elsewhere in this same round. -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\build\BuildOutputGuard.targets" />

</Project>
